+### Gradient Vector Flow Diffusion with CNN Denoising
+An alternative formulation that recovers grid-based vector fields from representative streamlines rather than direct grid upscaling [S1, unofficial]. It uses a two-stage approach: gradient vector flow diffusion followed by CNN-based denoising with recurrent residual blocks [S1, unofficial]. This method achieved 31–36 dB PSNR with compression rates of 12–51× on supercurrent, supernova, and tornado data [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct from CD-TVD in its input representation (streamlines vs. grids) and is chosen when data is available in streamline format rather than dense grids [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Performance:** On four fluid dynamics datasets at $4\times$ upscaling, CD-TVD achieved 43–48 dB PSNR [S1, unofficial].
--   **Comparison:** It outperformed GAN-based methods (such as ESRGAN, SSR-TVD, and STNet backbones) in terms of PSNR [S1, unofficial].
--   **Training Efficiency:** A key advantage is the ability to adapt to new simulation scenarios from a single high-resolution exemplar, significantly reducing the data requirement compared to methods requiring large datasets [S1, unofficial].
--   **Scalability:** Limited to volumes up to $1024^3$ per the reported studies [S1, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** High PSNR, few-shot adaptability, domain-specific feature learning via contrastive pre-training.
--   **Limitations:** Restricted volume size, dependency on representative single exemplar for fine-tuning, computational cost of 3D Swin Transformer inference.
+*   **Performance:** Achieves 43–48 dB PSNR on four fluid dynamics datasets at 4× upscaling [S1, unofficial].
